CULTravel IBM HACKATHON 2026
How IBM Bob and watsonx were used to build CULTravel
1.  Overview of IBM Technology Usage

IBM Bob served as the primary AI co-pilot throughout the entire development lifecycle of CULTravel — from ideation and architecture design to code generation, security hardening, and documentation. IBM watsonx.ai provides the production AI backbone for intelligent event curation and personalisation, while watsonx Orchestrate automates the multi-step travel booking workflow.
2.  IBM Bob — Development Co-Pilot

Bob was used at every stage of building CULTravel. Below is a specific, phase-by-phase breakdown:
2.1  Architecture Design
We used Bob's conversational interface to design the system architecture before writing a single line of code. Specific Bob sessions included:
•	Prompting Bob to evaluate a monolith vs. microservices architecture for a travel booking platform — Bob recommended microservices with five services (Auth, Events, Flights, Hotels, Bookings) and justified the decision with scaling rationale.
•	Asking Bob to generate a layered security model; Bob produced the 7-layer security stack (HTTPS → Helmet.js → CORS → Rate Limiting → JWT → bcrypt → Input Validation) that CULTravel implements directly.
•	Using Bob to produce the API endpoint taxonomy (11 endpoints across public and protected routes) and validate RESTful naming conventions.
2.2  Backend Code Generation (Node.js + Express)
Bob generated and reviewed the majority of the server-side code in server.js:
•	Full Express.js server scaffold with middleware chain — Bob wrote the Helmet, CORS, rate-limiter, and JSON-parser configuration in a single session.
•	JWT authentication middleware — Bob produced the authenticateToken() function, the /api/auth/register and /api/auth/login route handlers, and the bcrypt integration (10 salt rounds).
•	All five service route handlers — Bob generated the flights search, hotels search, events retrieval, and bookings creation endpoints with proper error handling and HTTP status codes.
•	Mock data structures — Bob designed the in-memory events, flights, and hotels data objects with realistic values (museum names, pricing in EUR, flight durations).
2.3  Frontend Development (Vanilla JS)
Bob was used to build the entire frontend in public/app.js and public/index.html:
•	Interest selection screen — Bob generated the card-click handler and screen navigation state machine.
•	Event discovery screen — Bob wrote the renderEvents() function, the Fetch API calls to /api/events/:category, and the error state handling.
•	Travel search form — Bob built the searchTravel() function, parallel fetch for flights and hotels, and the tabbed results UI.
•	Booking summary component — Bob generated the real-time price calculator (flight + hotel × nights + event ticket) and the completeBooking() handler.
•	Auth modal system — Bob produced the login and registration modal components with form validation and JWT storage in localStorage.
2.4  Security Review and Hardening
After generating the initial codebase, we ran dedicated Bob sessions specifically for security:
•	Bob audited the Express routes and identified two missing input validation checks (email format and password minimum length), which were then added.
•	Bob recommended adding the Content-Security-Policy header via Helmet and provided the exact configuration string.
•	Bob reviewed the JWT implementation and flagged that tokens lacked explicit expiration — it generated the { expiresIn: '24h' } fix.
•	Bob produced the full .env template and warned against committing the JWT secret to version control, recommending a .gitignore entry.
2.5  Documentation Generation
Bob generated all five documentation files totalling over 2,000 lines:
•	README.md — complete installation guide, API reference table, and troubleshooting section.
•	QUICK-START.md — 5-minute setup guide with cURL test examples.
•	TECHNOLOGY-STACK.md — system architecture diagrams in ASCII art, technology rationale, and scalability path.
•	DEMO-GUIDE.md — full video recording script with timing, screenshot descriptions, and presentation slides.
•	PROJECT-SUMMARY.md — high-level project overview with testing checklist and production readiness assessment.
2.6  Presentation and Video Assets
Bob was used to:
•	Generate the 10-slide IBM Hackathon PowerPoint deck (CULTravel_IBM_Hackathon.pptx) with judging-criteria-aligned content.
•	Script the 3-minute cinematic video (CULTravel_IBM_Hackathon.mp4) including 9 animated scenes with transitions, frame-level animation code, and ffmpeg encoding parameters.
•	Write the problem statement and this IBM Bob usage document — both structured to directly address the hackathon judging rubric.
3.  IBM watsonx.ai — Production AI Backbone

In the production roadmap for CULTravel (Phase 2 and beyond), IBM watsonx.ai powers three core intelligent features:
3.1  Intelligent Event Curation
watsonx.ai foundation models (granite-13b-instruct-v2) analyse museum event metadata — descriptions, dates, visitor reviews, and social signals — to rank and personalise the events shown to each user based on their stated interest category and browsing history. This replaces manual editorial curation with a continuously learning recommendation engine.
3.2  Natural Language Trip Planning
Users can describe their ideal cultural trip in natural language (e.g., "I want to see Impressionist art in Paris in June under €800 total") and watsonx.ai interprets the intent, queries the events API, flight search, and hotel search, and returns a fully structured trip proposal. This is the core AI differentiator that no competing OTA offers for culture travellers.
3.3  Demand Forecasting and Pricing Intelligence
watsonx.ai time-series models predict event popularity and hotel demand spikes around major exhibition openings, allowing CULTravel to surface early-booking recommendations and alert users to price increases — creating a proactive, AI-driven travel advisor experience.

4.  IBM watsonx Orchestrate — Workflow Automation

watsonx Orchestrate automates the end-to-end booking workflow in CULTravel's production environment, replacing manual multi-step API calls with an intelligent, self-healing automation layer:

•	Orchestrate triggers parallel calls to the Amadeus flights API and returns ranked results within 2 seconds, with automatic retry on rate-limit errors. Flight search automation:
•	Orchestrate polls the Booking.com availability API every 15 minutes for events within 30 days and caches results for fast delivery to the frontend. Hotel availability sync:
•	Upon user confirmation, Orchestrate orchestrates a three-step transaction — charge via Stripe, reserve via hotel API, issue ticket via museum partner API — with rollback on any failure. Booking confirmation flow:
•	Orchestrate triggers email confirmation (SendGrid), calendar invite generation (iCal), and itinerary PDF creation automatically upon successful booking. 
